#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:10
msgid ""
"1.4 The Renormalization Group: From Microscopic Spins to Macroscopic "
"Features"
msgstr ""
"1.4 The Renormalization Group: From Microscopic Spins to Macroscopic "
"Features"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:12
msgid ""
"So far we have seen how the Ising model describes a system of interacting "
"spins and how the Boltzmann distribution governs its equilibrium behaviour. "
"But what happens when we **zoom out, i.e.** when we stop looking at every "
"individual spin and instead focus on larger, coarse-grained blocks? This "
"question is answered by the **Renormalization Group (RG)**, a powerful "
"framework from statistical physics that explains why the laws of nature "
"appear simple at different scales and why seemingly different systems can "
"behave identically near a critical point. Remarkably, the RG also provides a"
" deep physical analogy for the hierarchical feature learning performed by "
"deep neural networks."
msgstr ""
"So far we have seen how the Ising model describes a system of interacting "
"spins and how the Boltzmann distribution governs its equilibrium behaviour. "
"But what happens when we **zoom out, i.e.** when we stop looking at every "
"individual spin and instead focus on larger, coarse-grained blocks? This "
"question is answered by the **Renormalization Group (RG)**, a powerful "
"framework from statistical physics that explains why the laws of nature "
"appear simple at different scales and why seemingly different systems can "
"behave identically near a critical point. Remarkably, the RG also provides a"
" deep physical analogy for the hierarchical feature learning performed by "
"deep neural networks."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:14
msgid "The Core Idea: Coarse-Graining"
msgstr "The Core Idea: Coarse-Graining"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:15
msgid ""
"Imagine a two-dimensional Ising model on a square lattice. Instead of "
"observing every spin, we partition the lattice into blocks of size $2 "
"\\times 2$, and replace each block with a single effective spin. This is "
"called **coarse-graining**. The new effective spins interact with each other"
" according to new, renormalized couplings $J'$ and fields $h'$. The key "
"insight is that the rules of the universe – the Hamiltonian – change when "
"you change your observational scale."
msgstr ""
"Imagine a two-dimensional Ising model on a square lattice. Instead of "
"observing every spin, we partition the lattice into blocks of size $2 "
"\\times 2$, and replace each block with a single effective spin. This is "
"called **coarse-graining**. The new effective spins interact with each other"
" according to new, renormalized couplings $J'$ and fields $h'$. The key "
"insight is that the rules of the universe – the Hamiltonian – change when "
"you change your observational scale."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:17
msgid "The process of coarse-graining is repeated iteratively:"
msgstr "The process of coarse-graining is repeated iteratively:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:19
msgid "Start with spins at the finest scale."
msgstr "Start with spins at the finest scale."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:20
msgid ""
"Apply a coarse-graining transformation (e.g., block averaging or majority "
"rule)."
msgstr ""
"Apply a coarse-graining transformation (e.g., block averaging or majority "
"rule)."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:21
msgid "Obtain a new, coarser Hamiltonian with new parameters."
msgstr "Obtain a new, coarser Hamiltonian with new parameters."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:22
msgid "Repeat."
msgstr "Repeat."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:24
msgid "This defines a **flow** in the space of Hamiltonians."
msgstr "This defines a **flow** in the space of Hamiltonians."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:26
msgid "Fixed Points and Universality"
msgstr "Fixed Points and Universality"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:27
msgid ""
"As the RG flow is iterated, the parameters $(J, h, \\ldots)$ move through "
"the parameter space. The system may eventually reach a **fixed point**– a "
"point where further coarse-graining leaves the Hamiltonian unchanged. Fixed "
"points correspond to **critical points** of the system, where the "
"correlation length diverges and the system becomes scale-invariant."
msgstr ""
"As the RG flow is iterated, the parameters $(J, h, \\ldots)$ move through "
"the parameter space. The system may eventually reach a **fixed point**– a "
"point where further coarse-graining leaves the Hamiltonian unchanged. Fixed "
"points correspond to **critical points** of the system, where the "
"correlation length diverges and the system becomes scale-invariant."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:29
msgid ""
"The profound consequence is **universality**: many different microscopic "
"systems (different metals, different liquids) flow to the same fixed point. "
"This is why we can compute properties of a liquid without knowing every "
"detail of the molecules – the relevant physics at the macroscopic scale is "
"determined by the fixed point, not by the microscopic details."
msgstr ""
"The profound consequence is **universality**: many different microscopic "
"systems (different metals, different liquids) flow to the same fixed point. "
"This is why we can compute properties of a liquid without knowing every "
"detail of the molecules – the relevant physics at the macroscopic scale is "
"determined by the fixed point, not by the microscopic details."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:31
msgid "The Renormalization Group and Deep Learning"
msgstr "The Renormalization Group and Deep Learning"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:32
msgid ""
"The RG provides a striking physical analogy for the **hierarchical feature "
"learning** performed by stacked RBMs (Deep Belief Networks, see Section [4.3"
" Beyond Single Layers: Stacking for Deep Learning](kpp-theoretical-"
"foundations-bm-deep.md))."
msgstr ""
"The RG provides a striking physical analogy for the **hierarchical feature "
"learning** performed by stacked RBMs (Deep Belief Networks, see Section [4.3"
" Beyond Single Layers: Stacking for Deep Learning](kpp-theoretical-"
"foundations-bm-deep.md))."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Renormalization Group"
msgstr "Renormalization Group"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Deep Belief Network (Stacked RBM)"
msgstr "Deep Belief Network (Stacked RBM)"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Coarse-grain spin blocks"
msgstr "Coarse-grain spin blocks"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Pooling / abstracting features"
msgstr "Pooling / abstracting features"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "New effective couplings"
msgstr "New effective couplings"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Higher-layer weights"
msgstr "Higher-layer weights"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Fixed point (scale invariance)"
msgstr "Fixed point (scale invariance)"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Top-level, most abstract representation"
msgstr "Top-level, most abstract representation"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid "Universality (same fixed point from many microstates)"
msgstr "Universality (same fixed point from many microstates)"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md
msgid ""
"Generalisation: same high-level features emerge from many training examples"
msgstr ""
"Generalisation: same high-level features emerge from many training examples"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:41
msgid ""
"In a DBN, each new hidden layer can be seen as a **coarse-graining "
"operation** on the distribution of features from the layer below. The first "
"hidden layer captures local edges and corners; the second layer combines "
"these into mid-level shapes; the third layer forms high-level object "
"representations. This process is directly analogous to an RG flow, where the"
" “microscopic” data distribution is progressively simplified into a "
"“macroscopic” abstract representation."
msgstr ""
"In a DBN, each new hidden layer can be seen as a **coarse-graining "
"operation** on the distribution of features from the layer below. The first "
"hidden layer captures local edges and corners; the second layer combines "
"these into mid-level shapes; the third layer forms high-level object "
"representations. This process is directly analogous to an RG flow, where the"
" “microscopic” data distribution is progressively simplified into a "
"“macroscopic” abstract representation."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:43
msgid ""
"Just as the RG reveals that all critical points belong to a finite number of"
" universality classes, deep learning shows that a well-trained network "
"automatically discovers the universal structure of the data – the features "
"that generalise across examples."
msgstr ""
"Just as the RG reveals that all critical points belong to a finite number of"
" universality classes, deep learning shows that a well-trained network "
"automatically discovers the universal structure of the data – the features "
"that generalise across examples."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:45
msgid "Extensive and Intensive Variables"
msgstr "Extensive and Intensive Variables"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:46
msgid ""
"To fully appreciate the RG analogy, we must distinguish between "
"**extensive** and **intensive** variables in thermodynamics:"
msgstr ""
"To fully appreciate the RG analogy, we must distinguish between "
"**extensive** and **intensive** variables in thermodynamics:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:48
msgid ""
"**Extensive variables** (e.g., volume $V$, energy $E$, entropy $S$, number "
"of particles $N$) scale with the size of the system."
msgstr ""
"**Extensive variables** (e.g., volume $V$, energy $E$, entropy $S$, number "
"of particles $N$) scale with the size of the system."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:49
msgid ""
"**Intensive variables** (e.g., temperature $T$, pressure $p$, chemical "
"potential $\\mu$) are independent of system size."
msgstr ""
"**Intensive variables** (e.g., temperature $T$, pressure $p$, chemical "
"potential $\\mu$) are independent of system size."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:51
msgid ""
"In the RG flow, the coarse-graining operation reduces the effective number "
"of degrees of freedom—the \"system size\" decreases. Intensive variables "
"like temperature and pressure remain invariant under scaling, while "
"extensive variables like energy and entropy scale linearly with the number "
"of effective spins."
msgstr ""
"In the RG flow, the coarse-graining operation reduces the effective number "
"of degrees of freedom—the \"system size\" decreases. Intensive variables "
"like temperature and pressure remain invariant under scaling, while "
"extensive variables like energy and entropy scale linearly with the number "
"of effective spins."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:53
msgid ""
"This distinction is mirrored in deep learning: high-level representations "
"are **intensive**—they capture invariant features of the data (e.g., "
"\"catness\") that are independent of the input's spatial resolution or pixel"
" count—while lower-level features are **extensive**, requiring more units to"
" represent fine-grained details."
msgstr ""
"This distinction is mirrored in deep learning: high-level representations "
"are **intensive**—they capture invariant features of the data (e.g., "
"\"catness\") that are independent of the input's spatial resolution or pixel"
" count—while lower-level features are **extensive**, requiring more units to"
" represent fine-grained details."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:55
msgid "The Connection to the Sampling Bottleneck"
msgstr "The Connection to the Sampling Bottleneck"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:56
msgid ""
"The RG also offers a physical perspective on the sampling problem introduced"
" in later sections. Near a critical point, the correlation length diverges, "
"and the system becomes difficult to simulate – Markov chains mix extremely "
"slowly (critical slowing down). This is precisely the difficulty encountered"
" when sampling from Boltzmann machines that are near their “critical” state,"
" i.e., when the model has learned a rich, multimodal distribution. "
"Understanding this slowdown is central to appreciating why alternative "
"approaches – such as quantum sampling – are needed."
msgstr ""
"The RG also offers a physical perspective on the sampling problem introduced"
" in later sections. Near a critical point, the correlation length diverges, "
"and the system becomes difficult to simulate – Markov chains mix extremely "
"slowly (critical slowing down). This is precisely the difficulty encountered"
" when sampling from Boltzmann machines that are near their “critical” state,"
" i.e., when the model has learned a rich, multimodal distribution. "
"Understanding this slowdown is central to appreciating why alternative "
"approaches – such as quantum sampling – are needed."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:58
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "Summary"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:59
msgid ""
"**Coarse-graining** transforms a system at one scale to an equivalent system"
" at a larger scale."
msgstr ""
"**Coarse-graining** transforms a system at one scale to an equivalent system"
" at a larger scale."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:60
msgid ""
"**Renormalization Group** is the study of how the Hamiltonian changes under "
"coarse-graining, leading to **fixed points** and **universality**."
msgstr ""
"**Renormalization Group** is the study of how the Hamiltonian changes under "
"coarse-graining, leading to **fixed points** and **universality**."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:61
msgid ""
"The RG provides a deep physical analogy for **hierarchical feature "
"learning** in deep neural networks: each layer coarse-grains the features "
"from the layer below."
msgstr ""
"The RG provides a deep physical analogy for **hierarchical feature "
"learning** in deep neural networks: each layer coarse-grains the features "
"from the layer below."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-stat-renormalization.md:62
msgid ""
"Critical slowing down in physical systems mirrors the **sampling "
"bottleneck** in energy-based models, motivating the search for more "
"efficient sampling methods."
msgstr ""
"Critical slowing down in physical systems mirrors the **sampling "
"bottleneck** in energy-based models, motivating the search for more "
"efficient sampling methods."

